Jira and GitLab Integration
Brief Integration Overview
Purpose. Integrate Jira and GitLab so that Jira Stories, Tasks, Bugs, and Defects can be traced to the related GitLab development activity, including branches, commits, and merge requests.
1. Integration Objective
Provide direct visibility of GitLab development activity from the related Jira work item.
Allow developers and reviewers to move between Jira and GitLab without manually searching repositories.
Create traceability from a Jira requirement or defect to the code changes and merge request that implemented it.
2. Correlation Between Jira and GitLab
The Jira issue key, for example PAY-458, is used to associate GitLab activity with the Jira work item.
The Jira key may be referenced in a branch name, commit message, merge request title, or merge request description.
It is not universally mandatory to include the Jira key in every branch or every commit. The organization should define at least one reliable and consistently enforced correlation method.
3. Recommended Working Convention
GitLab object 	Recommendation 	Example 
Branch 	Jira key optional, based on naming policy 	feature/payment-validation or feature/PAY-458-payment-validation 
Commit 	Jira key optional unless commit-level traceability is required 	PAY-458 Fix payment validation 
Merge Request 	Jira key recommended for clear work-item linkage 	PAY-458 - Payment validation changes 
MR description 	Include Jira key or Jira link and change summary 	Implements PAY-458 
4. Information Available Through the Integration
Branches associated with the Jira issue.
Commits that reference the Jira issue key.
Merge requests associated with the Jira issue.
Merge request status, such as Open, Merged, or Closed.
Reviewer and approval information where supported by the selected Jira/GitLab integration.
Links from Jira to the relevant GitLab objects and from GitLab references back to Jira.
5. Integration Methods
Native integration: Configure the supported Jira-GitLab connector or application to display development information in Jira.
REST APIs: Use Jira and GitLab APIs when complete history, reporting, validation, or audit extraction is required beyond the standard user interface.
Webhooks: Use GitLab push, merge request, and pipeline events when custom near-real-time Jira updates are required.
6. Example
Jira issue: PAY-458 - Payment validation defect.
Branch: feature/payment-validation.
Commits: PAY-458 Fix validation logic; PAY-458 Add unit tests.
Merge request: PAY-458 - Payment validation fix.
Result: Jira displays or links the related GitLab development activity for PAY-458.
7. Key Decision
The Jira issue ID is not inherently mandatory in every GitLab commit or branch name.
If the organization requires exact commit-level traceability, placing the Jira key in each commit message is a strong control.
If linkage through the merge request is sufficient, the Jira key can be mandatory only in the MR title or description, while branch and commit naming remain optional.
The selected rule should be validated against the actual Jira and GitLab versions and enforced consistently through team standards or repository checks.
8. Recommended Minimum Standard
Every code change must be traceable to a Jira work item.
The Jira key should be mandatory in the merge request title or description.
Branch and commit references should remain optional unless audit requirements demand branch-level or commit-level traceability.
Use API-based reporting when a complete, long-term history is required.
Summary: The integration should provide reliable Jira-to-GitLab traceability without imposing unnecessary naming rules. The mandatory point is the traceability outcome; where the Jira key must appear is a governance decision based on the required level of detail.
